FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:3434 — `interpolation_at` picks the **innermost** valid brace pair containing the caret (it iterates `valid` in ascending `open` order and lets the last match win), but the parser's own rule in `src/parser.rs:2955-2999` takes the **first (outermost)** matched `{` whose contents parse as one expression and then skips the whole span (`index = scan + 1`). When an outer interpolation is itself valid and contains a complete-expression brace pair (e.g. a record expression, `say "{ {name: 1} }"`), tooling selects the inner record-brace instead of the interpolation: `goto_definition_in_interpolation`/`find_references_in_interpolation` then build a view by replacing the whole literal with `name: 1` (src/lsp.rs:2705-2707, 2949-2951), `analyze` fails, and navigation silently answers `None`/empty instead of resolving names in the real expression. This is an untested nested-valid-pair branch (no fixture in tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs exercises an outer-valid interpolation containing an inner-valid pair). Concrete fix: select the outermost valid pair containing the caret (or reproduce `interpolated_parts`' left-to-right skip) rather than the innermost; add a fixture for a record/nested expression inside `{…}`. Not run: this is traced from the two selection loops; whether `{name: 1}` (or any nested pair) is simultaneously a complete expression determines reachability, so a fixture would settle it.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 1
